From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 15:55:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: Take a TLS close without close_notify as the end of a
 close-delimited body

An HTTP/1 response with neither a `Content-Length` nor a chunked
`Transfer-Encoding` ends when its connection does. Over TLS, rustls reports a close without
close_notify as an `UnexpectedEof` error, which the decoder only let through
once a `Content-Length` worth of bytes had been read. A close-delimited body
that the server closed without close_notify, as wptserve does, so ended with
an error although all of it had arrived.

Fetch treated that error like a body cut short, which marked the response
as aborted but still delivered the whole body with a successful end of
file. Take such a close as the end of the body instead, as Chromium and
Firefox do, although RFC 9112 section 9.8 calls the response incomplete. A
body with a `Content-Length` or a chunked coding still fails when the
connection closes early. The framing follows hyper, which reads the body:
a `Transfer-Encoding` takes precedence over a `Content-Length`, and one
whose last coding is not chunked is read until the connection closes. The HTTP cache, which reads the body below the
decoder, still does not store these responses.

The only other change is that devtools now gets the response, which it got
only for bodies that ended cleanly. The next patch stops keeping the bytes
of a body that is cut short, which would otherwise fail subresource
integrity for these responses.

Validation: `./mach test-unit -p servo-net`: 483 tests pass. `test_body_that_ends_with_its_tls_connection_without_close_notify_is_complete` fails without the change to the decoder: the response is marked as aborted. It also covers a `Transfer-Encoding` that does not end with chunked, with and without a `Content-Length`, which fails when any transfer coding counts as framing. `test_truncated_body_that_ends_with_its_tls_connection_is_aborted` covers a short `Content-Length` and chunked bodies without their last chunk, written `Chunked` or as the last coding of a second `Transfer-Encoding` line; a case-sensitive or first-line check for chunked fails it. WPT `subresource-integrity/integrity-policy/script.https.html`, where wptserve sends `common/dispatcher/dispatcher.js` this way, logs "finished response for" (the error path) for it without this patch.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent found the cause while checking WPT results of the next patch, wrote this patch and its tests, and applied the findings of independent reviews of this patch and of a later one)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
